\begin_layout Section
Empirical and Theoretical Results Leading to Conjecture 1
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Before proceeding, we establish some terminology and notation that will
 be used throughout the remainder of this work.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Le 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathfrak{H}_{n}:=\{w\in\mathfrak{H},\left|w\right|=n\}$
\end_inset

 denote the set of words of weight 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

.
 We recall that the set of admissible words is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}=x\mathfrak{H}y$
\end_inset

, and we denote the subset of admissible words of weight 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 by 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{n}:=x\mathfrak{H}_{n-2}y$
\end_inset

.
 For any admissible word 
\begin_inset Formula $w\in\mathcal{A}$
\end_inset

, we define its binary value, denoted by 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{bin}(w)$
\end_inset

 , as the unique even natural number whose binary representation yields
 the word 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 upon replacing each digit 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and each digit 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

. Equivalently, if a word is written as 
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{w=u_{1}u_{2}\dots u_{k}}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{u_{i}\in\{x,y\}}$
\end_inset

 then 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\ensuremath{\mathrm{bin}(w)=\sum_{i=1}^{k}c_{i}2^{k-i}},\text{ where }\ensuremath{c_{i}=1}\text{ if }\ensuremath{u_{i}=x}\text{, and }\ensuremath{c_{i}=0}\text{ if }\ensuremath{u_{i}=y.}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For example, consider the admissible word 
\begin_inset Formula $w=xxyy$
\end_inset

.
 Replacing each 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset

 gives the binary string 
\begin_inset Formula $1100_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Converting this to base 10, we obtain: 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{bin}(xxyy)=1\cdot2^{3}+1\cdot2^{2}+0\cdot2^{1}+0\cdot2^{0}=8+4=12.$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We will use a graded lexicographic total order on the words of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathfrak{H}$
\end_inset

, defined as follows.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 are words with 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|u\right|<\left|v\right|$
\end_inset

 , set 
\begin_inset Formula $u<v$
\end_inset

 .
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|u\right|=\left|v\right|$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u=wyw_{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $v=wxw_{3}$
\end_inset

 , for any words 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula $w_{1}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{2}$
\end_inset

, set 
\begin_inset Formula $u<v$
\end_inset

 .
 It is easy to see that the order on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}$
\end_inset

 induced by the mapping 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{bin(\,\cdot\,)}$
\end_inset

 coincides with the graded lexicographic order restricted to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}$
\end_inset

.
 The words of degree at most 
\begin_inset Formula $3$
\end_inset

 are thus ordered as follows:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
1<y<x<yy<yx<xy<xx<yyy<yyx<yxy<yxx<xyy<xyx<xxy<xxx
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For any subset 
\begin_inset Formula $V\subseteq\mathfrak{H}$
\end_inset

 and any integer 
\begin_inset Formula $m\ge1$
\end_inset

, let 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial_{m}(V)$
\end_inset

 denote the set 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\partial_{m}(w):w\in V\}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Conjecture 1 is based on the following analysis of the rank growth, which
 chronologically preceded the formulation of Conjecture
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

2.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For a fixed 
\begin_inset Formula $n\ge3$
\end_inset

, we will construct a full row-rank matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{M}_{n}$
\end_inset

 from the derivatives 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial_{n-\vert w\vert}(w)$
\end_inset

 of the elements 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 in the subset
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathcal{B}_{n}=\mathcal{A}_{n-1}\cup x\mathcal{A}_{n-3}\cup x\mathcal{A}_{n-2}\cup\dots\cup x\mathcal{A}_{2}\cup\mathcal{A}_{2}\subset h
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\left(\left|\mathcal{B}_{n}\right|=2^{n-3}+(2^{n-5}+2^{n-6}+\cdots+2^{0})+1=3\cdot2^{n-4}\right)$
\end_inset

 according to the following procedure: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The columns of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{M}_{n}$
\end_inset

 are indexed by the elements of the set of admissible words of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{n}$
\end_inset

, ordered ascendingly with respect to the graded lexicographic order.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $w=x^{n-2}y$
\end_inset

 be the maximal element of the set 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{n-1}$
\end_inset

.
 The first row of the matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{M}_{n}$
\end_inset

 is defined as the coordinate vector of the derivative 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial_{1}(w)\in\partial_{1}(\mathcal{A}_{n-1})\subset\text{Span}(\mathcal{A}_{n})$
\end_inset

 relative to the ordered basis 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{A}_{n},<)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
We then proceed iteratively through the remaining elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{n-1}$
\end_inset

 in descending order.
 For each element, we compute the coordinate vector of its derivative and
 insert it as a new row in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{M}_{n}$
\end_inset

.
 
\emph on
Crucially, throughout this process, we ensure that if a candidate row can
 be expressed as a linear combination of the preceding rows, it is discarded
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
This procedure is then repeated analogously for the elements of the remaining
 subsets in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{B}_{n}$
\end_inset

 (namely 
\begin_inset Formula $x\mathcal{A}_{n-3},x\mathcal{A}_{n-2},\dots,x\mathcal{A}_{2}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{2}$
\end_inset

 in the given order), examining their corresponding derivatives in descending
 order within each subset.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Constructing the matrix via this elimination process, it is evident that
 the resulting matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{M}_{n}$
\end_inset

 will have full row-rank.
 At the end of the procedure, we count how many derivatives originating
 from the subsets 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{n-1},x\mathcal{A}_{n-3},x\mathcal{A}_{n-2},\dots,x\mathcal{A}_{2}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{2}$
\end_inset

 remain as rows in the matrix.
 These quantities will be denoted by 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{0,n-2},r_{1,n-2},r_{2,n-2},\dots,r_{n-3,n-2}$
\end_inset

 respectively, and they will constitute the entries of the 
\begin_inset Formula $(n-2)$
\end_inset

-th column of an upper triangular matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{R}$
\end_inset

, whose rows and columns are indexed starting from 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since our JavaScript application, which includes the implementation of the
 above procedure, generates a clear graphical layout for smaller values
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

, the visual output representing the matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{M}_{6}$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $n=6$
\end_inset

 is included below as an illustrative example.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename Rank6.png
	width 96text%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For 
\begin_inset Formula $n=6$
\end_inset

, the generating set is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{B}_{6}=\mathcal{A}_{5}\cup x\mathcal{A}_{3}\cup x\mathcal{A}_{2}\cup\mathcal{A}_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 As clearly observed from the visual output, all 
\begin_inset Formula $2^{3}=8$
\end_inset

 elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial_{1}\left(\mathcal{A}_{5}\right)$
\end_inset

 and all 
\begin_inset Formula $2^{1}=2$
\end_inset

 elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial_{2}\left(x\mathcal{A}_{3}\right)$
\end_inset

 remain in the matrix.
 Conversely, the elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial_{3}\left(x\mathcal{A}_{2}\right)=\{\partial_{3}(xxy)\}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial_{4}\left(\mathcal{A}_{2}\right)=\{\partial_{4}(xy)\}$
\end_inset

 can already be expressed as linear combinations of the preceding rows and
 are thus discarded.
 By definition, this yields 
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{r_{0,4}=8},\,\ensuremath{r_{1,4}=2}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{2,4}=r_{3,4}=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To provide an additional illustration, let us consider the output generated
 for the case 
\begin_inset Formula $n=7$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
