	filename Rank7.png
	width 96text%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
From this output, it can be observed that all 
\begin_inset Formula $16$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $4$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $2$
\end_inset

 elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial_{1}\left(\mathcal{A}_{6}\right)$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial_{2}\left(x\mathcal{A}_{4}\right)$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial_{3}\left(x\mathcal{A}_{3}\right)$
\end_inset

, respectively, remain in the matrix, whereas all elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial_{4}\left(x\mathcal{A}_{2}\right)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial_{5}\left(\mathcal{A}_{2}\right)$
\end_inset

 are eliminated.
 By definition, this yields 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{0,5}=16$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{1,5}=4$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{2,5}=2$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{3,5}=r_{4,5}=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Next, we present the empirical results obtained for 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i,j}$
\end_inset

 from 
\begin_inset Formula $n=2$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $n=17$
\end_inset

, collected into the table below:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{array}{cc|ccccccccccccccccc}
{\color{lightgray}} & {\color{lightgray}n=} & {\color{lightgray}2} & {\color{lightgray}3} & {\color{lightgray}4} & {\color{lightgray}5} & {\color{lightgray}6} & {\color{lightgray}7} & {\color{lightgray}8} & {\color{lightgray}9} & {\color{lightgray}10} & {\color{lightgray}11} & {\color{lightgray}12} & {\color{lightgray}13} & {\color{lightgray}14} & {\color{lightgray}15} & {\color{lightgray}16} & {\color{lightgray}17} & {\color{lightgray}18}\\
