 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $j\geq1$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A formalization of the recurrence relation observed in the table is given
 by:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
r_{i,j}=\begin{cases}
0 & \text{if }i<0\text{ or }j<0;\\
1 & \text{if }i=j\text{ for }i=0,1,2,3,\dots;\\
2^{\max(0,j-1)} & \text{if }i=1\text{ for }j=0,1,2,3,\dots;\\
a_{i-1,j-1}-a_{i-1,j-i-1} & \text{otherwise.}
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The recurrence relation was investigated within a more general theoretical
 framework as a transformation.
 Instead of the initial values 
\begin_inset Formula $2^{\max(0,j-1)}$
\end_inset

 entered into the first row, let us consider an arbitrary arithmetic sequence
 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{a}=(a(1),a(2),\dots)$
\end_inset

, and let the column sums define the values of the transformed sequence
 
\begin_inset Formula $T(\boldsymbol{a})=(T(a(1)),T(a(2)),\dots)$
\end_inset

.
 During our thorough theoretical investigation, it soon became apparent
 that this transformation is remarkably versatile and well-suited for providing
 concise and clear explanations of the connections between various combinatorial
 results with a special emphasis on pentagonal formulas.
 Furthermore, since it is based on shifted differences and a summation,
 the transformation is inherently linear.
 What is essential for our purposes is that the transformation maps the
 sequence 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{a}=(1,0,0,0,\dots)$
\end_inset

 to the sequence 
\begin_inset Formula $-\boldsymbol{\omega}=(1,1,0,0,-1,0,-1,0,0,0,0,1,0,\dots)$
\end_inset

 (now, starting from 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 rather than 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset

 as done above).
 Consequently, by linearity, the transform of an arbitrary sequence 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{a}=(a_{1},a_{2},a_{3},\dots)$
\end_inset

 yields a pentagonal formula of the form 
\begin_inset Formula $-\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}\omega(k)a_{n-k}$
\end_inset

, which can be written more concisely as 
\begin_inset Formula $T(\boldsymbol{a})=-(a_{1},a_{2},\dots)*(\omega(1),\omega(2),\dots)$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $*$
\end_inset

 denotes the convolution of sequences.
 This, along with a few straightforward algebraic manipulations, directly
 recovers the pentagonal formula presented in Conjecture 1.
 Since the specific areas discussing this type of transformation lie beyond
 the current scope of our knowledge, we shall adopt the term 
\emph on
pentagonal transformation
\emph default
 for the remainder of this work.
 During the study of the transformation, it also became apparent that when
 transforming various sequences 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{a}=(a(1),a(2),\dots)$
\end_inset

, it is not only the transformed sequence 
\begin_inset Formula $T(\mathbf{a})$
\end_inset

 itself that is of interest; the individual rows generated in the table
 during the underlying recurrence relation, as well as their corresponding
 generating functions, are also highly significant.
 In the table above leading to the values of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathscr{L}(j)$
\end_inset

, for instance, it is clearly observable that the first non-zero entries
 of successive rows coincide with the sequence 
\begin_inset Formula $(\text{pic}(0),\text{pic}(1),\text{pic}(2),\dots)=(1,0,0,1,2,5,9,19,37,\dots)$
\end_inset

 for increasingly longer stretches.
 From this, together with certain relations to be discussed in Section 5,
 it will become completely evident that each row of the table contains parametri
c generalizations 
\begin_inset Formula $(\text{pic}_{n}(0),\text{pic}_{n}(1),\text{pic}_{n}(2),\dots)$
\end_inset

 of the sequence 
\begin_inset Formula $(\text{pic}(0),\text{pic}(1),\text{pic}(2),\dots)$
\end_inset

 corresponding to the parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 of that row.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As for the proof of Conjecture 1, it suffices to demonstrate two conditions:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
for any 
\begin_inset Formula $n\geq3$
\end_inset

, the vectors in the set 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial_{1}(\mathcal{A}_{n-1})$
\end_inset

 are linearly independent; 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
the rank increments 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i,j}$
\end_inset

 follow the aforementioned recurrence relation.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The first statement can be straightforwardly established without any difficulty,
 from which the corresponding power of two naturally follows.
 However, we have been unable to provide a proof for the second statement.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It is worth emphasizing that the credibility of Conjecture 1 is significantly
 reinforced by the fact that the consistency does not merely rest on the
 first fourteen terms of a single sequence.
 Rather, it is deeply embedded in the entire underlying table of rank increments
, matching perfectly across the whole two-dimensional recursive structure.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Empirical and Theoretical Results Leading to Conjecture 2
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The empirical results leading to Conjecture 2 can be obtained via the exact
 same elimination procedure described for Conjecture
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

1; however, rather than traversing the words in descending order, the matrix
 is iteratively expanded by examining the coordinate vectors of their derivative
s in ascending order.
 This modification offers a distinct computational advantage: as can be
 readily verified, the coordinate vectors of the derivatives in the set
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathcal{B}_{n}^{*}=\mathcal{B}_{n}\setminus\mathcal{A}_{n-1}=\partial_{n-2}(\mathcal{A}_{2})\cup\partial_{n-3}(x\mathcal{A}_{2})\cup\dots\cup\partial_{2}(x\mathcal{A}_{n-3})
\]

\end_inset

relative to the ordered basis 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{A}_{n},<)$
\end_inset

 are guaranteed to form a linearly independent system of vectors.
 Consequently, for any fixed 
\begin_inset Formula $n\ge3$
\end_inset

, the elimination procedure can be initiated immediately with the full row-rank
 matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{N}_{n}$
\end_inset

 whose rows are defined as the coordinate vectors of the derivatives contained
 in the set 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{B}_{n}^{*}$
\end_inset

 without requiring any prior rank computation.
 This initial matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{N}_{n}$
\end_inset

 consists of exactly 
\begin_inset Formula $1+(2^{0}+2^{1}+\cdots+2^{n-6}+2^{n-5})=2^{n-4}$
\end_inset

 rows.
 Subsequently, we iterate through the remaining elements of the set 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{n-1}$
\end_inset

 in ascending order, starting from its minimal element, 
\begin_inset Formula $xy^{n-2}$
\end_inset

.
 For each successive word 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

, we append the coordinate vector of its derivative 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial_{1}(w)$
\end_inset

 as a candidate row to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{N}_{n}$
\end_inset

; if this candidate row increases the rank of the matrix, it is retained,
 and otherwise, it is discarded.
 Upon completion of this procedure, the resulting matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{N}_{n}$
\end_inset

 is inherently full row-rank, just like the matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{M}_{n}$
\end_inset

 discussed in Conjecture 1.
 Furthermore, the ranks of these two matrices are identical.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since our custom JavaScript application—which implements the aforementioned
 procedure too—generates a clear graphical layout for small values of 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

, the visual outputs representing the matrices 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{N}_{6}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{N}_{7}$
\end_inset

 are included below as illustrative examples.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename Rank6Le.png
	width 96text%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename Rank7Le.png
	width 96text%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Although it is theoretically possible that a word 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{1}\in\mathcal{A}_{n-1}$
\end_inset

 is skipped while the derivative of a larger word 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{2}>w_{1}$
\end_inset

 is retained in the matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{N}_{n}$
\end_inset

, empirical evidence suggests that this never occurs for 
\begin_inset Formula $n>6$
\end_inset

.
 Specifically, for any weight 
\begin_inset Formula $n\neq6$
\end_inset

, there exists a unique critical threshold word 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{\text{crit}}\in\mathcal{A}_{n-1}$
\end_inset

 such that its derivative is discarded, and consequently, the derivatives
 of all subsequent words 
\begin_inset Formula $w>w_{\text{crit}}$
\end_inset

 are also eliminated.
 In short, with the sole exception of 
\begin_inset Formula $n=6$
\end_inset

, elements are systematically discarded exclusively from the tail of the
 ordered set 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{n-1}$
\end_inset

.
 (The anomalous behavior observed in the case 
\begin_inset Formula $n=6$
\end_inset

 is explicitly accounted for by the "congestion" of the pentagonal numbers
 3 and 5.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In light of these observations, 
\emph on
Conjecture 2 can be stated in a sharper form as follows
\emph default
: for any weight 
\begin_inset Formula $n\neq6$
\end_inset

, during the construction of the matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{N}_{n}$
\end_inset

, the first and minimal word 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{0}\in\mathcal{A}_{n-1}$
\end_inset

 whose derivative fails to increase the rank of the matrix is precisely
 the 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(3\cdot2^{n-4}-\text{pic}(n-1)\right)$
\end_inset

-th element in the ordered set.
 Consequently, the derivatives of all words larger than 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{0}$
\end_inset

 likewise fail to increment the rank of the matrix.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $|\mathcal{B}_{n}^{*}|=2^{n-4}$
\end_inset

, and exactly 
\begin_inset Formula $3\cdot2^{n-4}-\text{pic}(n-1)-1$
\end_inset

 elements from 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{n-1}$
\end_inset

 are retained in the matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{N}_{n}$
\end_inset

, it follows that: 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathscr{L}(n)=\text{rank}(\mathbf{N}_{n})=2^{n-4}+3\cdot2^{n-4}-\text{pic}(n-1)-1=2^{n-2}-\text{pic}(n-1)-1,
\]

\end_inset

which precisely recovers the formulation of Conjecture 2 presented in Section
 2.
 Although the equivalence between Conjecture 1 and Conjecture 2 can be formally
 established with ease in light of the identities to be discussed in Section
 5 (where we shall demonstrate that the rank increments are fundamentally
 parametric generalizations of pure inverting compositions), we consider
 it essential to present these two approaches separately, as the structural
 nuances arising between them are not fully captured by the explicit formulas
 alone.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It is worth noting that this particular formulation and matrix construction
 are highly optimized for computationally efficient implementations, which
 could potentially enable the verification of the conjecture for weights
 well beyond 
\begin_inset Formula $n=17$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It is highly instructive to reflect upon a comment by N.
 J.
 A.
 Sloane in the OEIS entry for 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{A178841}$
\end_inset

, where he noted that the striking similarity between the terms of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{A178841}(n)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{A152537}(n)$
\end_inset

 might be "just a coincidence, caused by the fact that their generating
 functions are almost identical." Our framework provides a rigorous, deeper
 theoretical explanation that dispels this assumption of coincidence.
 The connection between these two sequences is fundamentally rooted in the
 dual matrix constructions presented herein.
 Specifically, building the derivation matrix in descending order yields
 a rank-increment structure governed by 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{A152537}$
\end_inset

 (Conjecture 1), whereas building it in ascending order yields a structure
 governed by 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{A178841}$
\end_inset

 (Conjecture
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

2).
 Since both procedures ultimately resolve the exact same system of Ihara-Kaneko\SpecialChar nobreakdash
Z
agier derivation relations, the ranks of the resulting matrices 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{M}_{n}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{N}_{n}$
\end_inset

 must identically coincide with 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathscr{L}(n)$
\end_inset

, thereby intrinsically forcing the near-identity of their respective generating
 functions.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Generalization of 
\begin_inset Formula $\text{pic}(n)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
\align center

\color gray
Preliminary draft.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this section, we demonstrate that the rank increments can be viewed as
 a natural generalization of the classical sequence 
\begin_inset Formula $\text{pic}(k)$
\end_inset

 to the parametric function 
\begin_inset Formula $\text{pic}_{n}(k)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Before presenting the formal identities, we define three fundamental families
 of polynomials that govern these combinatorial structures.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{Fib}_{n}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{Fab}_{n}$
\end_inset

 denote the multivariate Fibonacci and Faber partition polynomials, respectively.
 Furthermore, let 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{Z}_{n}$
\end_inset

 denote the cycle index polynomial of the symmetric group 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{n}$
\end_inset

.
 For a given set of variables 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n})$
\end_inset

, these polynomials are explicitly defined as follows: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\mathrm{Fib}_{n}(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots x_{n}) & = & \sum_{1b_{1}+2b_{2}+\cdots+nb_{n}=n}(-1)^{b_{1}+\cdots+b_{n}}\left(b_{1}+\cdots+b_{n}\right)!\;\frac{x_{1}^{b_{1}}x_{2}^{b_{2}}\cdots x_{n}^{b_{n}}}{b_{1}!\,b_{2}!\cdots b_{n}!}\\
\mathrm{Fab}_{n}(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots x_{n}) & = & \sum_{1b_{1}+2b_{2}+\cdots+nb_{n}=n}(-1)^{b_{1}+\cdots+b_{n}}\,n\,\left(b_{1}+\cdots+b_{n}-1\right)!\;\frac{x_{1}^{b_{1}}x_{2}^{b_{2}}\cdots x_{n}^{b_{n}}}{b_{1}!\,b_{2}!\cdots b_{n}!}\\
\mathrm{Z}_{n}(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots x_{n}) & = & \sum_{1b_{1}+2b_{2}+\cdots+nb_{n}=n}\dfrac{1}{1^{b_{1}}2^{b_{2}}\cdots n^{b_{n}}}\;\frac{x_{1}^{b_{1}}x_{2}^{b_{2}}\cdots x_{n}^{b_{n}}}{b_{1}!\,b_{2}!\cdots b_{n}!}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Motivated by the theoretical results established in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "claesson2023permutationsinversions"
literal "true"

\end_inset

, we present the fundamental recurrence relation satisfied by 
\begin_inset Formula $\text{pic}(k)$
\end_inset

, along with several formulas that characterize the intricate relations
 among the pure inverting composition function 
\begin_inset Formula $\text{pic}(k)$
\end_inset

, the partition function 
\begin_inset Formula $p(k)$
\end_inset

, and the divisor sum function 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma(k)$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\text{pic}(k) & = & \sum_{j=0}^{k}\omega(j)\,2^{k-1-j}+\omega(j)\\
k\,\text{pic}(k) & = & \sum_{j=0}^{k}\text{pic}(k-j)\,\left(2^{j}-\sigma(j)-1\right)\\
\text{pic}(k) & = & 2\,\text{pic}(k-1)+\omega(k)-\omega(k-1)\\
\text{pic}(k) & = & \text{Z}_{k}(2^{1}-\sigma(1)-1,2^{2}-\sigma(2)-1,\ldots,2^{k}-\sigma(k)-1)\\
\sigma(k)-2^{k}+1 & = & \text{Fab}_{k}(\text{pic}(1),\text{pic}(2),\ldots,\text{pic}(k))\\
\sum_{j=0}^{k}p(j)-2p(k) & = & -\text{Fib}_{k}(\text{pic}(1),\text{pic}(2),\ldots,\text{pic}(k))\\
\text{pic}(k) & = & \text{Fib}_{k}\left(2p(1)-\sum_{j=0}^{1}p(j),2p(2)-\sum_{j=0}^{2}p(j),\ldots,2p(k)-\sum_{j=0}^{k}p(j)\right)\\
p(k) & = & \sum_{j=0}^{k}2^{\max(0,k-1-j)}\,\text{Fib}_{j}(\text{pic}(1),\text{pic}(2),\ldots,\text{pic}(j))
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
These relationships can be naturally generalized by restricting the underlying
 arithmetic functions.
 Specifically, let 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{{\scriptscriptstyle >}n}(k)$
\end_inset

 denote the number of partitions of 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 into parts strictly greater than 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

, and let 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{n}(k)$
\end_inset

 denote the restricted divisor sum function, representing the sum of divisors
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 that are at most 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

.
 By adopting the first formula below as the formal definition of the generalized
 function 
\begin_inset Formula $\text{pic}_{n}(k)$
\end_inset

, all the preceding identities can be seamlessly mapped to this generalized
 setting as follows: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\text{pic}_{n}(k) & := & \text{Z}_{k}(2^{1}-\sigma_{n}(1)-1,2^{2}-\sigma_{n}(2)-1,\ldots,2^{k}-\sigma_{n}(k)-1)\\
k\,\text{pic}_{n}(k) & = & \sum_{j=1}^{k}\text{pic}_{n}(k-j)\,\left(2^{j}-\sigma_{n}(j)-1\right)\\
\text{pic}_{n}(k) & = & \text{pic}_{n-1}(k)-\text{pic}_{n-1}(k-n)\\
\text{pic}_{n}\left(\binom{n+1}{2}+k\right) & = & 2^{k-1}\left[2\,\text{pic}_{n}\left(\binom{n+1}{2}\right)-(-1)^{n}\right]\\
\sigma_{n}(k)-2^{k}+1 & = & \text{Fab}_{k}(\text{pic}_{n}(1),\text{pic}_{n}(2),\ldots,\text{pic}_{n}(k))\\
\sum_{j=0}^{k}p_{{\scriptscriptstyle >}n}(j)-2p_{{\scriptscriptstyle >}n}(k) & = & -\text{Fib}_{k}(\text{pic}_{n}(1),\text{pic}_{n}(2),\ldots,\text{pic}_{n}(k))\\
\text{pic}_{n}(k) & = & \text{Fib}_{k}\left(2p_{{\scriptscriptstyle >}n}(1)-\sum_{j=0}^{1}p_{{\scriptscriptstyle >}n}(j),2p_{{\scriptscriptstyle >}n}(2)-\sum_{j=0}^{2}p_{{\scriptscriptstyle >}n}(j),\ldots,2p_{{\scriptscriptstyle >}n}(k)-\sum_{j=0}^{k}p_{{\scriptscriptstyle >}n}(j)\right)\\
p_{{\scriptscriptstyle >}n}(k) & = & \sum_{j=0}^{k}2^{\max(0,k-1-j)}\,\text{Fib}_{j}(\text{pic}_{n}(1),\text{pic}_{n}(2),\ldots,\text{pic}_{n}(j))
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Furthermore, the following connection holds between the newly introduced
 generalized function 
\begin_inset Formula $\text{pic}_{n}(k)$
\end_inset

 and the classical sequence 
\begin_inset Formula $\text{pic}(k)$
\end_inset

: 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sum_{k=0}^{n}\text{pic}_{n-k}(k)=2^{n}-\text{pic}(n+1)\,\,(n=0,1,2,\ldots).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For all 
\begin_inset Formula $n\geq0$
\end_inset

 let 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega_{n}(k)$
\end_inset

 (cf.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "OEIS:A231599"
target "https://oeis.org/A231599"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
