\begin_layout Section
The Pentagonal Transform and Its Recurrence Matrix
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 denote the left shift operator acting on sequences, defined by 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
S((a_{n})_{n\ge0})=(a_{n+1})_{n\ge0}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For each 
\begin_inset Formula $k\ge0$
\end_inset

, let 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{e}_{k}=(e_{k}(n))_{n\ge0}$
\end_inset

 denote the 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

-th standard basis sequence, defined via the Kronecker delta by 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{k}(n):=\delta_{k,n}\quad(n\ge0).$
\end_inset

 In particular, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{e}_{0}=(1,0,0,\dots)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{e}_{1}=(0,1,0,\dots)$
\end_inset

, where the only non-zero term of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{e}_{k}$
\end_inset

 appears at index 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

.
 We will frequently use throughout this chapter the sequence 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{\delta}=(\delta(n))_{n\ge0}$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbf{\delta}:=S(\mathbf{e}_{0}-\mathbf{\omega})=(1,1,0,0,-1,0,-1,0,0,0,0,1,0,0,1,0,\dots).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
That is, 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta(n)=$
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "A257628(n+1)"
target "https://oeis.org/A257628"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $(n\geq0)$
\end_inset

.
 We retain the notation for the vectors 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{\omega}_{n}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{p}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{p}_{{\scriptscriptstyle \leqq}n}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{p}_{{\scriptscriptstyle >}n}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{pic}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{pic}_{n}$
\end_inset

 established in the previous chapter.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{a}=(a(n))_{n\ge0}$
\end_inset

 be a fixed sequence with 
\begin_inset Formula $a(0)=1$
\end_inset

.
 We associate with 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{a}$
\end_inset

 an infinite two-dimensional matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{R}(\mathbf{a})=(r(i,j))$
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset Formula $i,j\in\mathbb{Z}$
\end_inset

) by the following recurrence relation:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
r(i,j)=\begin{cases}
0 & \text{if }i<0\text{ or }j<i,\\
a(j) & \text{if }i=0\text{ and }j\ge0,\\
r(i-1,j-1)-r(i-1,j-1-i) & \text{otherwise.}
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

The 
\emph on
pentagonal transform
\emph default
 of the sequence 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{a}$
\end_inset

, denoted by 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{P}(\mathbf{a})$
\end_inset

 is the sequence 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{b}=(b(n))_{n\ge0}$
\end_inset

 whose 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

-th term is given by the sum of the elements in the 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

-th column of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{R}(\mathbf{a})$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
b(n):=\sum_{i=0}^{n}r(i,n)\quad(n\ge0).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It is an immediate consequence of the definition that the transform 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{P}$
\end_inset

 is linear.
 The pentagonal transform 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{P}$
\end_inset

 and its underlying recurrence relation possess widespread applications;
 in fact, the wealth of combinatorial identities derivable from them could
 fill an entire volume on their own.
 For our present purposes, however, it suffices to note that for any arbitrary
 sequence 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{a}$
\end_inset

, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathcal{P}(\mathbf{a})=\mathbf{\delta}*\mathbf{a},
\]

\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $*$
\end_inset

 denotes the convolution of sequences.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To illustrate the mechanics of the pentagonal transform 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{P}$
\end_inset

 and the structure of its associated matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{R}(\mathbf{a})$
\end_inset

, we present two concrete examples generated via a custom JavaScript application.
 Since these matrices are inherently infinite, the figures display only
 their first few rows and columns.
 The first displays matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{R}(\mathbf{e}_{0})$
\end_inset

 corresponding to the standard basis sequence 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{e}_{0}=(1,0,0,\dots)$
\end_inset

 (indicated in the top blue row).
 As expected, the resulting column sums (highlighted in the bottom green
 row) precisely yield the sequence 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{\delta}$
\end_inset

, reflecting the property 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{P}(\mathbf{e}_{0})=\mathbf{\delta}$
\end_inset

.
 Similarly, the second demonstrates the transform applied to the sequence
 of partition numbers 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{p}=(p(n))_{n\ge0}$
\end_inset

.
 In this case, the top blue row contains the initial partition values, while
 the bottom green row displays the row sums constituting the transformed
 sequence 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{P}(\mathbf{p})=S(\mathbf{p})$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

