\begin_layout Standard
If 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{1}$
\end_inset

 denotes the constant sequence 
\begin_inset Formula $(1,1,1,\dots)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{L}$
\end_inset

 denotes the sequence 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{L}(2),\mathcal{L}(3),\mathcal{L}(4),\dots)$
\end_inset

, then this relationship can be expressed in the following compact form
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbf{L}=\mathcal{P}(\mathbf{a})-\mathbf{1}=\delta*\mathbf{p}*\mathbf{pic}-\mathbf{1},
\]

\end_inset

from which the formula stated in Conjecture 1 can be obtained via basic
 algebraic manipulations.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
However, the connection runs much deeper; it manifests not only at the level
 of the transformed sequence 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{L}=\mathcal{P}(\mathbf{a})-\mathbf{1}$
\end_inset

, but also throughout the entire recurrence matrix, appearing in every single
 row.
 Consequently, this observation opens a clear avenue toward proving Conjecture
 1.
 To elucidate this deeper connection, we introduce a so-called rank-increment
 matrix associated with the derivation relation, and compare its empirically
 observed values with the matrix of the recently discussed transform.
 In alignment with the notation established in Chapter 3, let 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{n}:=x\mathfrak{H}_{n-2}y$
\end_inset

 denote the set of admissible words of length 
\begin_inset Formula $n\geq2$
\end_inset

, and let 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial_{m}(\mathcal{A}_{n}):=\{\partial_{m}(w):w\in\mathcal{A}_{n}\}$
\end_inset

 for any 
\begin_inset Formula $m\geq1$
\end_inset

.
 For notational convenience, we index the rows of the rank-increment matrix
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{R}=(r_{m,n})$
\end_inset

 starting from 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 and its columns starting from 
\begin_inset Formula $2$
\end_inset

, so that 
\begin_inset Formula $m\ge1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $n\ge2$
\end_inset

.
 The entries 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{n,m}$
\end_inset

 of the rank-increment matrix are formally defined as follows: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The entry in the first row and 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

-th column is given by the rank of the vector system 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial_{1}(\mathcal{A}_{n-1})$
\end_inset

; that is, 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
r_{1,n}=\text{rank}\left(\partial_{1}(\mathcal{A}_{n-1})\right)\quad\text{for all }n\ge2.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
For the subsequent rows where 
\begin_inset Formula $m\ge2$
\end_inset

, the entry 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{m,n}$
\end_inset

 is defined as the rank increment obtained by adjoining the vector system
 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial_{m}(\mathcal{A}_{n-m})$
\end_inset

 to the combined system 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bigcup_{k=1}^{m-1}\partial_{k}(\mathcal{A}_{n-k}).
\]

\end_inset

 That is,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
r_{m,n}=\text{rank}\left(\bigcup_{k=1}^{m}\partial_{k}(\mathcal{A}_{n-k})\right)-\text{rank}\left(\bigcup_{k=1}^{m-1}\partial_{k}(\mathcal{A}_{n-k})\right)
\]

\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $m\ge2$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $n\ge m+1$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This formulation is completely equivalent to the rank-increment procedure
 discussed in Chapter 3; however, while the focus here is on conceptual
 clarity and conciseness, the description there was tailored to precisely
 reflect the algorithmic implementation.
 By exploiting the underlying duality, for rows where 
\begin_inset Formula $2\leq m\leq n-3$
\end_inset

, we restricted our analysis to the vector systems 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial_{m}(x\mathcal{A}_{n-m-1})$
\end_inset

, which comprise only half of the full system 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial_{m}(\mathcal{A}_{n-m})$
\end_inset

.
 Furthermore, those elements were adjoined to the matrix sequentially, one
 by one, according to a fixed ordering, discarding those that did not increase
 the rank.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The empirically computed rank-increment matrix is presented below, exhibiting
 a perfect match with the strictly upper triangular part of the transformation
 matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{R}(\mathbf{a})$
\end_inset

 initialized with the sequence 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{a}=(1,1,2,4,8,16,\dots)$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

